Current bottleneck picture
- Hard deopts are largely eliminated on key case-tree workloads.
- Speedups are now real on several heavy programs (notably `mat_mul`, `nqueens`, `ray_sphere`, `sort_merge`) but far from admission target (~10x each).
- Biggest remaining structural blockers are likely architectural, not residual counters:
  - compiled path still allocates/constructs many dynamic nodes in expression/case-tree paths,
  - compiled recursion still uses C call depth caps (`CALL_DEPTH_CAP` seen on `gab_deriv`).

Operational lessons
- `--as-c` temp artifacts are still non-parallel-safe (`/tmp/hvm4-aot-as-c.main.*` race); keep telemetry serialized.
- `bench.ts` exits non-zero when any row times out; still use final printed table for comparisons.

Next likely steps
1. Run deopt/resid telemetry on the full admission set (not just canaries) to verify where compiled execution is still escaping.
2. Remove remaining constructor-field residualization (`EXPR_CTR_FIELD_CAPTURE`) with bounded structural policy.
3. Prototype recursion-depth mitigation (tail-call conversion first, then trampoline/dispatcher if needed).
4. Start a deeper emitter redesign for truly allocation-light case-tree execution (consume APP frames directly; avoid rebuilding obvious spine terms).
